On Nov 4, 2:27 am, Jistan Idiot <jistanid...@gmail.com> wrote:
> On Nov 3, 7:31 pm, sam.f...@gmail.com wrote:
> > So I rolled a dwarven valkyrie and after exploring most of
> > the first level, I entered a room with 5 jackals which
> > proceeded to kill me within a few turns. My cat didn't lift
> > a finger to help and I only managed to kill one of them
> > before I died. Quaffing a potion didn't do anything to help.

> Yep that will happen.  Run away.  Never take on more than one
> monster at a time.  The person who suggested running into a
> corridor so only one could attack at a time has an excellent
> idea.  You can also send in kitten first, although pets are
> not as helpful as one might hope.

It depends.  They're not dependable, that's for sure.  But like
you, they become stronger with the monsters they kill (and eat);
jackals are fine for this, since killing them doesn't up your
level much, but is as good as anything else for a pet.  And
given time, even the smallest kitten will attack (and kill) a
jackal.  So often, in such cases, I'll just scratch out the E
word, even if I could easily kill them.

Also, unless you're a priest, you definitely want to keep a pet
around until you find an altar, for trying on armor.

    [...]
> > I explored as far as I can around the level. However, this
> > time the first level seems much much smaller, with only
> > three rooms connected by two corridors. There are no stairs
> > down and no more doors of any kind. I'm walking around but
> > can find nothing more to do, no objects, no monsters and no
> > stairs down, what do I do in this position?

> With very very rare exceptions there is always a downstairs
> (and that only happens a lot deeper in the dungeon).  Check
> out the "s" (search) command.  Use it a lot in this
> situation.

With experience, of course, you'll more or less know the most
likely places to check.  But even then, there can be exceptions;
in my current game, on one of the early levels, I must have
needed well over 50 's' at different placed before finding a way
to advance.  (That's very rare, however.)

